## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of time-based wakeups, simple data structures, and race conditions in OS scheduling.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel data structure and boundary wrapping
- Tick advancement, wakeup path, and race-free enqueues
- Concurrency: locking or per-bucket synchronization

## Code Example
```c
struct SleepRef { int thread_id; int wake_tick; struct SleepRef* next; };
#define WHEEL_SIZE 256
struct SleepRef* wheel[WHEEL_SIZE];
int current_tick = 0;
```

```c
void add_sleep(int ticks_from_now, int thread){
  int t = (current_tick + ticks_from_now) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  lock_bucket(t);
  insert_tail(&wheel[t], thread);
  unlock_bucket(t);
}
```

```c
void tick_handler(void){
  current_tick = (current_tick + 1) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  int idx = current_tick % WHEEL_SIZE;
  lock_bucket(idx);
  wake_all_in_bucket(wheel[idx]);
  wheel[idx] = NULL;
  unlock_bucket(idx);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Understanding safe concurrent data sharing is fundamental for OS-level IPC. This task tests practical use of atomics, memory ordering, and race testing.

## Key Concepts

- Ring buffer basics, head/tail invariants
- Atomic operations: CAS, fetch_add, acquire/release
- Memory ordering and visibility on multi-core CPUs
- Overflow handling and backpressure

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t head;
  _Atomic uint32_t tail;
  int slots[256];
} ring_t;

bool enqueue(ring_t *r, int val){
  uint32_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h >= 256) return false; // full
  r->slots[t & 255] = val;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(ring_t *r, int *out){
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&r->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return false; // empty
  *out = r->slots[h & 255];
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

